// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`new-project prep prompt (static) > snapshot — fully static, no input 1`] = `
"NEW-PROJECT PREP — output a "new-project" payload to CREATE a new iBuild4you project.
The first key of your JSON output MUST be \`"_payload_type": "new-project"\`.
\`title\` is required. Include \`requester_email\`, \`requester_first_name\`, \`requester_last_name\` if known — these drive maker membership and email approval.

I'm a project builder on iBuild4you setting up a new intake project. Help me think through the setup: who the maker is, what we know about the idea, what the agent's first session should focus on, and any seed questions or directives that would help.

When I say "give me the output", produce ONLY valid JSON matching this schema (no markdown, no code fences). Use straight ASCII quotes only (" and '), never curly/smart quotes, and no non-breaking spaces. \`_payload_type\` MUST be the first key.

{
  "_payload_type": "new-project",
  "title": "Project name shown in the dashboard (required)",
  "requester_email": "Maker's email. Required to share the project with them later — creates a maker membership and approves the email for sign-in.",
  "requester_first_name": "Maker's first name",
  "requester_last_name": "Maker's last name",
  "language": "en or fr. The language the maker is addressed in (agent replies, outbound messages, link previews). Omit for English.",
  "context": "Background info the agent uses to skip basic discovery questions. Multi-sentence is fine.",
  "welcome_message": "Posted word for word as the agent's first message when the maker opens the project for the first time. Sets tone.",
  "nudge_message": "Optional. Verbatim outbound nudge text used to invite the maker back. Omit to let the system auto-draft.",
  "voice_sample": "Optional. One paragraph showing how the builder texts this person by hand — anchors AI-generated outbound copy.",
  "identity": "Optional. Custom agent persona override. Omit unless the builder explicitly wants a non-default persona.",
  "session_mode": "discover (broad exploration) or converge (narrow toward decisions). Defaults to discover when omitted.",
  "seed_questions": ["Questions the agent should weave into the first session. Used only in discover mode."],
  "builder_directives": ["Instructions injected into the agent's system prompt — e.g. 'focus on the ordering workflow'. Used only in converge mode; ignored in discover mode. Guardrails that must hold in every session (e.g. 'do not suggest technologies') belong in context, not builder_directives."],
  "brief": {
    "problem": "What problem the maker is trying to solve. Omit the brief entirely if not enough is known yet.",
    "target_users": "Who the intended users are",
    "features": ["Feature 1", "Feature 2"],
    "constraints": "Any constraints, limitations, or things the maker explicitly doesn't want",
    "additional_context": "Any other relevant information that doesn't fit the above categories",
    "decisions": [{ "topic": "short label", "decision": "what was decided" }],
    "open_risks": ["plain-language description of something unresolved, unclear, or risky", { "text": "a risk only one side can settle", "owner": "maker" }],
    "shipped": [{ "date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "items": ["something already built and available to the maker"] }],
    "queued": ["something planned but not built yet"]
  }
}

Rules:
- Only include top-level fields we've actually discussed. Omit fields with no signal — don't invent a \`nudge_message\` or \`voice_sample\` from thin air.
- \`session_opener\` is accepted as a legacy alias for \`welcome_message\` — prefer \`welcome_message\`.
- If the maker should be addressed in a language other than English, set \`language\` (e.g. "fr") and write \`welcome_message\` and \`nudge_message\` in that language. The language setting makes the agent use it from the first message, so don't add a directive for it. The brief itself always stays in English (the builder reads it): translate anything the maker said in another language.
- \`title\` is required; everything else is optional.
- Omit the entire \`brief\` object if not enough is known to seed it — the agent will discover during the first session.
- Extract brief content ONLY from what we discussed. Do not invent or assume.
- Features should be distinct, actionable items (not vague goals).
- A decision = the builder/maker committed to a specific choice (not just mentioned an option).
- Open risks = things that are unresolved, unclear, or risky. Examples: "no plan for getting first users", "pricing model undecided". A risk may carry \`"owner": "maker"\` when only the maker can settle it (their preference, a fact about their world, a decision that is theirs to make) or \`"owner": "builder"\` when it is the builder's to resolve (technical, scheduling, something the builder said they would look into); omit \`owner\` when unclear."
`;

exports[`next-convo prep prompt — permute the inputs that actually flow in > snapshot — brief present (no decisions), 1 session 1`] = `
"NEXT-CONVO PREP — output a "next-convo" payload for an EXISTING project.
The first key of your JSON output MUST be \`"_payload_type": "next-convo"\`.
Do NOT include \`title\`, \`requester_email\`, or maker names — those are create-only fields.

I'm a project builder managing an intake project. I need your help preparing for the next session with my maker (the person whose app idea we're exploring), and curating the project's brief.

Your curation task: rewrite the brief whole, from the current brief plus every note below. After each conversation with the maker, the app writes a short note of what that conversation added (decisions, new open risks, risks settled, and any request to reverse a locked decision). Fold every note into the brief, so that the brief alone says everything the notes say. Be terse. You are free to merge entries and drop history and superseded detail. Keep the brief under 20,000 characters of JSON; its current size is given in the <current_brief> block. The brief's readers are agents, not people: Sam (the intake agent that talks to the maker), the comms agent (you), and Claude Code when the build starts. The curation guide at the end says how.

When I say "give me the output", produce ONLY valid JSON matching this schema (no markdown, no code fences). Use straight ASCII quotes only (" and '), never curly/smart quotes, and no non-breaking spaces. \`_payload_type\` MUST be the first key.

{
  "_payload_type": "next-convo",
  "_prep_stamp": { "brief_version": 0, "note_ids": [] },
  "brief": {
    "problem": "What problem the user is trying to solve",
    "target_users": "Who the intended users are",
    "features": ["Feature 1", "Feature 2"],
    "constraints": "Any constraints, limitations, or things the user explicitly doesn't want",
    "additional_context": "Any other relevant information that doesn't fit the above categories",
    "decisions": [{ "topic": "short label", "decision": "what was decided", "decided_in_session": "optional session id", "decided_at": "optional ISO timestamp", "revised_in_session": "optional session id", "revised_at": "optional ISO timestamp" }],
    "open_risks": [{ "text": "plain-language description of something unresolved, unclear, or risky", "owner": "maker" }],
    "shipped": [{ "date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "items": ["something built and available to the maker"] }],
    "queued": ["something planned but not built yet"]
  },
  "welcome_message": "Posted word for word as the agent's first message when the maker opens the next session, so include any link or step they need. Omit it to let the agent greet them itself.",
  "nudge_message": "Optional. Verbatim outbound nudge text used to invite the maker back. Omit to let the system auto-draft.",
  "voice_sample": "Optional. One paragraph showing how the builder texts this person by hand — anchors AI-generated outbound copy.",
  "identity": "Optional. Custom agent persona override for this project. Omit unless the builder explicitly wants a non-default persona.",
  "context": "Optional. Background the agent uses to skip basic discovery questions. Update only if new background surfaced in the conversation.",
  "session_mode": "discover (broad exploration) or converge (narrow toward decisions)",
  "seed_questions": ["Questions the agent should weave into the early part of the next session. Used only in discover mode."],
  "builder_directives": ["Instructions injected into the agent's system prompt to steer the conversation — e.g. 'push toward a decision on auth flow'. Used only in converge mode; ignored in discover mode. Guardrails that must hold in every session belong in context."]
}

Rules:
- \`_prep_stamp\`: copy the JSON from the <prep_stamp> block exactly, unchanged, as a top-level key (the values in the schema above are placeholders). The app uses it to fold exactly the notes you were given and to warn if the brief moved on after this prep was made.
- Always include the whole \`brief\`, rewritten: every field, even unchanged ones. Anything you leave out of it is gone from the brief.
- For the other top-level fields, include only what our discussion actually addressed. Omit the rest — don't invent a \`nudge_message\` or \`voice_sample\` from thin air.
- \`session_opener\` is accepted as a legacy alias for \`welcome_message\` — prefer \`welcome_message\`.
- The maker's language is given in the <project> block. Write \`welcome_message\` and \`nudge_message\` in that language. The project's language setting already makes the agent use it, so don't add a directive for it. The brief itself always stays in English, because the agents that read it (Sam, the comms agent, Claude Code) work in English: translate anything the maker said in another language.
- Build the brief ONLY from the current brief and the notes. Do not invent or assume.
- If a brief field has no information yet, use an empty string (or empty array for features/decisions/open_risks). \`shipped\` and \`queued\` may be omitted when empty.
- Carry \`shipped\` and \`queued\` forward: they are part of the whole brief, so anything you leave out is gone. Move dated "we shipped X" history out of \`additional_context\` into \`shipped\` as \`{ "date", "items" }\` batches. When a note says a queued item shipped, remove it from \`queued\` and add it to the latest \`shipped\` batch.
- Features should be distinct, actionable items (not vague goals).
- A decision = the user committed to a specific choice (not just mentioned an option). Each decision has a short topic label and the decision itself.
- A decision answers the question it was given. Keep it within that question's scope: don't turn an answer about one feature into a general rule, or into a separate decision. Use the maker's own words, quoting them when they're short. Don't record consequences or follow-on changes the maker didn't state.
- Keep every decision still in force. A note's decision on a topic the brief already has is a revision: the decision keeps its \`topic\` text exactly as on the brief (the app matches provenance by topic) and only \`decision\` changes.
- A decision may carry \`"locked": true\` — a durable constraint (e.g. a locked convention or do-not-use rule). NEVER drop, reword, or unset a locked decision; carry it forward verbatim with \`"locked": true\`. When a note says the maker asked to reverse a locked decision, that request is already on the brief as \`contested\` (the app put it there when it wrote the note): carry \`contested\` and \`contest_dismissed\` exactly as they appear, and never add, change or remove them yourself. A builder decides whether the lock changes.
- A decision may carry \`decided_in_session\` / \`decided_at\` and \`revised_in_session\` / \`revised_at\` — provenance stamps. Carry them through unchanged; never invent them (the system stamps new and revised decisions itself).
- Open risks = things that are unresolved, unclear, or risky. Every open risk carries \`"owner": "maker"\` when only the maker can settle it (their preference, a fact about their world, a decision that is theirs to make) or \`"owner": "builder"\` when it is the builder's to resolve (technical, scheduling, something the builder said they would look into). Carry an existing owner forward. Drop a risk a note marks as settled.

BRIEF CURATION GUIDE

Who reads the brief
- Sam, the intake agent, reads it at the start of every conversation with the maker: it is how Sam knows what is settled, what not to ask again, and where to steer.
- The comms agent (you, when you curate) reads it to prepare each next conversation.
- Claude Code reads it when the build starts.
No person reads it as a document. Write for those agents: terse, factual, no narrative.

Size
- Keep the brief JSON (the "brief" object, serialized) under 20,000 characters. Smaller is better: the whole brief goes into every chat turn.
- Near the budget, merge and drop before you add.

Keep
- What Sam needs so it does not ask the maker again: facts about the maker's world, their preferences, every decision still in force.
- What Sam needs to steer: open risks, gaps, what the maker is unsure about.

Merge
- Duplicates and near-duplicates (the same feature or fact raised in different conversations) into one entry.
- A feature and its refinements into one feature.

Drop
- History: who said what and when, how a decision was reached, earlier wording. The app keeps every past version.
- Detail a later decision superseded.
- Open risks a note marks as settled.

Decisions
- A locked decision ("locked": true) is copied verbatim, every field. Never reword it, never drop it, never unset the lock.
- When a note records that the maker asked to reverse a locked decision, the app has already put that on the brief as "contested". Carry "contested" and "contest_dismissed" exactly as they appear; do not change the decision, the lock or the contest yourself. The builder settles it.
- When a note revises a decision, keep that decision's "topic" text exactly as it is on the brief and change only "decision". The app matches decisions by topic; a reworded topic reads as a new decision and loses its history.
- Keep each decision within the question it answered, in the maker's own words.
- Carry decided_in_session, decided_at, revised_in_session and revised_at through unchanged.

Open risks
- Every open risk is an object with "text" and "owner": "maker" when only the maker can settle it (their preference, a fact about their world, a choice that is theirs), "builder" when it is the builder's to resolve (technical, scheduling, something the builder said they would look into).
- Carry an existing owner forward. Give an ownerless risk the owner that fits.

Shipped and queued
- "shipped" is dated batches of things already built: { "date": "YYYY-MM-DD", "items": [...] }. Keep every batch; only when the size budget presses, merge or drop the oldest first.
- A dated "we shipped X" passage in additional_context belongs in "shipped". Move it there.
- "queued" is a list of things planned but not built. When a note says a queued item shipped, remove it from "queued" and add it to the latest "shipped" batch.
- Always carry "shipped" and "queued" forward: a brief written without them loses them.

Language
- The brief is in English whatever language the maker speaks, because the agents that read it work in English. Translate the maker's words.

Please review the project data the builder will give you next and let's discuss before you produce the output:
- What went well in the conversations so far?
- What should the next session focus on?
- Should we stay in discover mode or switch to converge?

<project>
Title: Sam's Cafe
Sessions so far: 1
Maker's language: English
</project>

<current_brief version="1">
Current size: 159 characters (budget 20,000)
{
  "problem": "no online ordering",
  "target_users": "cafe customers",
  "features": [
    "catalog"
  ],
  "constraints": "",
  "additional_context": "",
  "decisions": [],
  "open_risks": []
}
</current_brief>

<notes>
No new notes since the brief was last curated.
</notes>

<prep_stamp>
{"brief_version":1,"note_ids":[]}
</prep_stamp>"
`;

